chapter('The person and the room','Situations & attribution','room','#216B88',
 'Given an ambiguous action, generate two situational explanations and identify evidence that distinguishes them from a personal explanation.',
 'Maya arrives late. The room has already decided why.',
 'A neighborhood team is turning an empty shop into a repair café. At the first meeting, Maya arrives twenty minutes late. Leo takes her lateness as a sign that she doesn’t care about the project.\n\nWhat Leo hasn’t seen is the broken lift at Maya’s apartment, or the neighbor she helped down the stairs.\n\nLeo’s conclusion—that Maya didn’t care—was one possible explanation for her lateness. But he hadn’t considered what might have delayed her.\n\nFirst, examine Leo’s reasoning in this fictional story. What did Leo observe about Maya’s arrival, and what did he infer about her priorities?\n\nThen think of a recent occasion when you explained someone’s behavior. What did you observe directly, and which cause did you infer?\n\nThroughout this course, keep that distinction in mind.',
 [co('Behavior has more than one author.',['Person: tendencies, goals, beliefs, skills', 'Disposition: a usual tendency, such as patience', 'Situation: demands, people, available choices', 'Interaction: pressure can strain usual patience'],
 'A disposition is a personal tendency that persists over time. Someone described as having a “sweet disposition” is generally kind and good-natured. A tendency need not appear in every situation.\n\nA situation is the set of circumstances someone faces, including other people, immediate demands, and available choices. A usually patient volunteer might speak sharply when several visitors demand help at once. Both the volunteer’s usual patience and the pressure matter.\n\nSocial psychology studies how people’s thoughts, feelings, and behavior are shaped by other people and social circumstances. That includes how personal qualities and circumstances interact. Personal qualities include dispositions, but also goals, beliefs, and skills.\n\nFor Maya, the broken lift made arriving on time harder. Investigating that barrier helps the team explain her lateness and plan future shifts, while keeping commitments clear.'),
 co('Observation is not explanation.',['Observation: Maya arrived after the start.', 'Attribution: Leo explains it as not caring.', 'Fundamental attribution error: too much weight on the person, too little on the situation.', 'Check: compare behavior across situations.'],
 'When you explain another person’s behavior, you can give too much weight to personal qualities and too little to circumstances. Psychologists call this tendency the fundamental attribution error. Its strength varies with culture and context.\n\nIn the story, Leo observed that Maya arrived after the start. His claim that “Maya doesn’t care” was an explanation of her lateness, rather than something he directly observed. The broken lift and helping a neighbor offer alternative causes for the delay.\n\nWhen you explain someone’s behavior in your own life, begin by describing what you observed without adding a cause. Then generate alternative explanations and ask what evidence would weaken each one, including the explanation you first favored. These steps help you test your own interpretation.')],
 'An attribution is an explanation of what caused an action.\n\nAttribution research describes patterns across judgments. Those patterns do not settle the cause of one person’s action.\n\nActor–observer differences compare how one observer explains their own actions and other people’s actions. In a hypothetical comparison, Leo might cite a bus delay for his own lateness but Maya’s priorities for hers. The pattern varies with the judgment and study design.',
 q('Leo is assigning the opening shift. What is his most informative next move?', ['Ask Maya about today and check her availability.','Use today’s lateness as the main sign of reliability.','Ignore today and assume she will arrive on time.'],0,['Asking Maya about the delay lets Leo investigate the obstacle and plan the shift without ignoring the lateness.','One episode of lateness is a weak guide to how reliably someone usually arrives.','Assuming Maya will arrive on time does not investigate today’s delay or check her future availability.']),
 q('A volunteer misses shifts only when the bus service is suspended. What should you test?', ['Whether personality alone predicts attendance.','Whether another travel plan improves attendance.','Whether the missed shifts can be deleted.'],1,['Focusing on personality alone leaves the link between bus cancellations and missed shifts untested.','Trying another travel plan tests the suspected barrier while keeping the attendance expectation clear.','Deleting the missed shifts from the record would hide the pattern. It would not remove the travel barrier.']),
 ['In Maya’s arrival story, what did Leo observe, and what cause did he infer?','What evidence would strengthen an explanation based on a person’s usual tendencies, rather than one unusual situation?'],['Leo observed that Maya arrived after the meeting had started. He inferred that she did not care about the project. Her late arrival is the observation; not caring is his proposed cause.','An explanation based on a personal tendency becomes more credible when the behavior recurs across varied circumstances and plausible alternatives have been examined.'],
 'Choose a mildly frustrating action by another person. Write what you observed, your initial explanation, two plausible alternatives, and one respectful question that could clarify what happened.',
 [os('12-1-what-is-social-psychology','What Is Social Psychology?')])
chapter('The poster that “worked”','Evidence & experiments','experiment','#7058A3',
 'Identify a confound in a before-and-after claim and propose a fairer comparison.',
 'A brighter poster. More visitors. A cause?',
 'Leo replaces the café’s plain poster with a colorful one. Attendance rises the next weekend. Leo credits the new poster for the increase.\n\nBut other things changed too. The rain stopped, the local paper ran a story, and the opening hours changed.\n\nThe team knows that more people attended. The team does not yet know how much the poster contributed. Keep that distinction in mind whenever someone says that one change caused another.',
 [co('Comparison makes a claim testable.',['Correlation: things vary together.', 'Confound: another factor could explain the pattern.', 'Random assignment: allocate conditions by chance.', 'Random sampling: select people from a population by chance.'],
 'Correlation means that two things vary together. A confound is another factor that could explain an apparent relationship. Attendance rose after Leo changed the poster, but better weather could also have brought more visitors. The poster comparison cannot separate those explanations.\n\nAn experiment deliberately changes a condition and compares outcomes. For one café event, a chance process could assign households to receive invitation A or B. This random assignment helps balance other influences between groups on average; it cannot guarantee identical groups.\n\nRandom sampling concerns who enters the study. Selecting households by chance from a neighborhood list is different from assigning invitation designs to households already selected. Testing only existing supporters may tell us little about residents who have never visited.'),
 co('Ask how much—and how uncertain.',['Size: 12% versus 15% is a difference of three percentage points.', 'Uncertainty: another sample may give a different estimate.', 'Statistical significance: judged against a model, often assuming no group difference.', 'Significance does not establish practical importance; nonsignificance does not prove zero effect.'],
 'Imagine two groups of one hundred households. Twelve attend after receiving one invitation design. Fifteen attend after receiving the other. These are invented teaching numbers.\n\nAttendance is twelve percent in one group and fifteen percent in the other: a difference of three percentage points. A different sample could produce a different result, so the estimated advantage has uncertainty.\n\nStatistical significance concerns how unusual a result would be under a specified statistical model, often one assuming no difference between groups. It does not tell us whether a difference matters in practice. A small change could be statistically significant in a large study, yet be too small to justify an expensive campaign.\n\nA nonsignificant result does not prove zero effect. Examine the size of the estimate, its uncertainty, the study design, and other tests.')],
 'Replication repeats a test to examine how dependable a finding is. Independent tests help us learn whether a result holds up elsewhere.\n\nPreregistration records the planned methods and analysis before results are known. That advance record reduces some opportunities for selective reporting. Preregistration cannot rescue a poor design.\n\nWith a sound design, a larger sample can narrow the uncertainty around an estimate. More observations cannot fix a systematically unfair comparison. Sharing the study’s methods and materials lets others inspect how it was done. A memorable study is a starting point for evaluation, rather than a final verdict.',
 q('Which statement fits Leo’s evidence?', ['The poster caused the increase.','The poster had no effect.','Attendance rose; several causes remain possible.'],2,['Crediting the poster alone overlooks the weather, publicity, and opening hours that changed at the same time.','Uncertainty about the poster’s contribution does not establish that the poster had no effect.','Saying that attendance rose preserves the observation. Leaving the cause open reflects the limits of the comparison.']),
 q('Which invitation comparison is strongest?', ['Design A in winter; design B in summer.','Randomize designs for one event; define attendance in advance.','Design A for existing fans; design B for newcomers.'],1,['Comparing winter with summer changes the season alongside the invitation design.','Randomizing invitations for one event improves the comparison. Also check whether households share invitations.','Existing fans and newcomers differ in prior interest. That difference could affect attendance regardless of the invitation.']),
 ['How does random assignment differ from random sampling?','Why does the rise in attendance after Leo changed the poster fail to establish that the poster caused the increase?'],['Random assignment allocates people to conditions. Random sampling selects people from a population.','Other conditions can change over time and produce the same before-and-after pattern.'],
 'Find an everyday causal claim. Name its outcome, one competing explanation, and a feasible comparison. State whose behavior the result would represent.',
 [os('2-3-analyzing-findings','Analyzing Findings'),no('the-replication-crisis-in-psychology','The Replication Crisis in Psychology')])
chapter('The story that comes to mind','Social cognition','filter','#A1486D',
 'Identify a missing base rate and formulate a question that could challenge an initial belief.',
 'One broken toaster becomes “they always break.”',
 'A repaired toaster fails again. The story spreads through the café chat. Leo concludes that electrical repairs usually fail, although nobody has counted the outcomes.\n\nMaya remembers grateful visitors and overlooks returns. Maya therefore has a more positive impression than Leo.\n\nBoth people remember genuine cases. Neither person has systematically checked all comparable repairs. To estimate how often repairs fail, the team needs a record that includes the cases nobody talked about.',
 [co('Memorable is not the same as common.',['Availability heuristic: easy-to-recall examples shape judgment.', 'Base rate: how common is it among comparable cases?', 'Denominator: total comparable repairs, including successes.'],
 'Heuristics are mental shortcuts. They can help when time and information are limited. The availability shortcut uses examples that come easily to mind. After hearing the toaster story, Leo may judge repair failures by how easily he remembers one.\n\nVivid or recent examples can be a poor guide to frequency. A complaint channel helps discover faults, but usually says little about repairs that worked.\n\nTo estimate a failure rate, count failures and the total of comparable repairs. In an invented example, four failures among forty repairs means ten percent. Forty is the denominator: the total used to interpret the four failures. Without that total and consistent follow-up, the café cannot tell how common failure is.'),
 co('Beliefs can filter their own evidence.',['Confirmation bias: seeking or interpreting evidence to fit an existing belief.', 'Motivated reasoning: what we want to be true influences our reasoning.', 'Check: what evidence could change your mind?'],
 'Confirmation bias means seeking or interpreting information in ways that fit an existing belief. If Leo thinks evening sessions fail, asking only about unsuccessful evenings gives his belief an unfair advantage.\n\nMotivated reasoning concerns how a conclusion we want to reach influences our reasoning. Maya wants the café to succeed, so she may find reassuring repair stories easier to accept. These processes can overlap; neither label proves that a conclusion is wrong.\n\nTo check your own reasoning, define the outcome before collecting results. Record comparable successes and failures. Ask someone with a different expectation to review that record.\n\nThese steps make it possible for evidence to challenge your initial belief.')],
 'When assessing someone else’s estimate, compare that estimate with a relevant record before proposing that it is biased. A bias label alone cannot establish how the person reached the estimate.\n\nFor example, Leo’s vivid repair stories might give a distorted impression of failure. In another case, an experienced repairer’s remembered cases might accurately reflect a dependable record. Representative evidence helps distinguish these possibilities.\n\nWhen reviewing another person’s reasoning, ask which information they used and which cases they missed. Disagreeing with their estimate does not replace that investigation. Experts also need feedback and opportunities to revise their judgments.',
 q('What most improves the estimate of repeat failures?', ['A detailed account of the worst failure.','Failures divided by comparable completed repairs.','A vote on whose impression feels realistic.'],1,['A failed repair can help explain how a fault occurs. One case cannot establish how often repairs fail.','The total of comparable repairs provides the denominator for a failure rate. Consistent follow-up still matters.','A vote measures the team’s beliefs. The voters may share the same gaps in information.']),
 q('Leo thinks evening sessions fail. Which question best challenges his belief?', ['Which evenings went badly?','Who agrees that evenings are a poor idea?','What outcomes would make us conclude evenings work?'],2,['Looking only at unsuccessful evening sessions selects evidence that fits Leo’s initial belief.','Agreement with Leo may reflect shared assumptions rather than independent evidence.','Naming outcomes that would count as success gives Leo’s belief a chance to be wrong.']),
 ['Why can’t Leo estimate the repair failure rate from one vivid toaster failure? What total must he count?','When can an availability shortcut be useful?'],['The denominator is the total of comparable cases. Four failed repairs mean something different among forty repairs than among four hundred. Consistent follow-up is needed to estimate the failure rate.','An availability shortcut can help when the examples that come to mind reliably represent the relevant events.'],
 'Keep a small record of one everyday prediction for a week. Include successes and failures. Avoid collecting private information about others. Compare the whole record with your first impression.',
 [os('7-3-problem-solving','Problem Solving'),no('social-cognition-and-attitudes','Social Cognition and Attitudes')])
chapter('The plan we cannot let go','Self & self-justification','dissonance','#9A5630',
 'Distinguish dissonance reduction from evidence-based revision and compare future options without counting irrecoverable costs as benefits.',
 '“I chose it” quietly becomes “it must be right.”',
 'Maya championed a booking system. After weeks of work, the system still loses reservations. Maya usually spots technical problems quickly, but now explains each failure away.\n\nFor Maya, admitting that the system is poor feels like admitting that she is poor at her role. Leo proposes another month of configuration because the team has already spent so much.\n\nMaya is defending her judgment. Leo is focused on money already spent. Both need to compare what the available options would deliver from today onward.',
 [co('Inconsistency can feel uncomfortable.',['Cognitive dissonance: inconsistency between beliefs or actions can feel uncomfortable.', 'Cognitions: beliefs and knowledge about our actions.', '“I choose reliable tools” clashes with a system that loses bookings.', 'Reduce discomfort by revising a belief or action, or adding a justification.'],
 'Cognitive dissonance theory concerns a clash between things we believe or know about our actions. “Cognitions” is the technical term for those beliefs and pieces of knowledge. The theory proposes that such a clash can create discomfort we are motivated to reduce.\n\nMaya believes she chooses reliable tools, yet the booking system she chose keeps losing reservations. She could reduce that discomfort by dismissing each failure, or by revising her view: a capable coordinator can recognize and correct a poor choice.\n\nPeople may change a behavior, change a belief, or add a justification. The response is not predetermined. How much choice and responsibility someone feels can affect the pressure to justify an action.'),
 co('Give the future a fresh vote.',['Sunk cost: money, time, or effort that cannot be recovered.', 'Sunk cost effect: letting that past expense drive the next choice.', 'Compare future benefits and costs, including switching and usable skills.'],
 'A sunk cost is money, time, or effort already spent that cannot be recovered. The sunk cost effect occurs when that past expense influences a decision about what to do next.\n\nThe hours spent configuring the booking system cannot be recovered by continuing to use it. The next decision concerns future bookings, support costs, and the effort needed to switch. Compare the future benefits and avoidable costs of each option.\n\nPast work can still matter through its future consequences. Skills the team can reuse are a benefit; retraining after a switch is a future cost. Those consequences belong in the comparison. The wish to make past effort feel worthwhile does not, by itself, favor continuing.')],
 'Evidence for specific cognitive dissonance demonstrations is uneven. In 2024, researchers repeated one procedure across multiple laboratories. Participants were asked to argue for a position they did not hold, with more or less choice about doing so. This is called an induced-compliance procedure.\n\nThe study tested whether the amount of choice affected later attitudes. Its primary analysis did not support the predicted difference between the higher-choice and lower-choice groups.\n\nThat result challenges the particular procedure tested. It does not settle every version of cognitive dissonance theory. Distinguish the broad explanation of discomfort and justification from the evidence for a particular prediction. The linked Vaidis and colleagues study provides the details.',
 q('What makes honest revision easier?', ['Review failures and compare options afresh.','Remind Maya that a competent coordinator would know better.','Keep the system until the investment feels worthwhile.'],0,['Reviewing failures and comparing options turns attention toward outcomes and the next decision.','Questioning Maya’s competence can increase defensiveness without improving the comparison of options.','Continuing with an inferior option cannot recover money that has already been spent.']),
 q('Switching costs £100; keeping the tool costs £300 in future support. Benefits are equal. What matters?', ['The large fee already paid.','The lower avoidable future cost of switching.','Only the emotional discomfort of switching.'],1,['The original fee cannot be recovered under the stated assumptions. It does not favor either future option.','Under the stated assumptions, switching costs one hundred pounds and keeping the tool costs three hundred. Real choices also compare risks.','Discomfort about switching may matter to the people involved, but it does not replace the comparison of future costs and benefits.']),
 ['Name two ways to reduce dissonance.','When can a past investment still matter?'],['People may reduce dissonance by changing a behavior or belief, or by adding a justification. Context affects the response.','A past investment matters when it changes future consequences, such as switching costs or usable skills.'],
 'Revisit a small commitment you can change. List the future benefits and avoidable costs of continuing or stopping. Write a sentence that permits correction without attacking your character.',
 [os('12-3-attitudes-and-persuasion','Attitudes and Persuasion'),dict(title='Vaidis et al. (2024): Multilab dissonance replication',url='https://doi.org/10.1177/25152459231213375'),os('7-3-problem-solving','Problem Solving')])
chapter('The quiet vote','Conformity & authority','crowd','#216B88',
 'Distinguish normative and informational influence and design a process that makes independent concerns visible.',
 'Everyone says yes. Has everyone agreed?',
 'A donor offers a publicity event. The chair calls it a wonderful opportunity, and the first three volunteers agree. Sam doubts that the team can manage the workload, but stays silent.\n\nSam assumes Maya has checked the risks. Maya reads Sam’s silence as reassurance. Each person is taking the other’s outward response as a sign that the proposal has been considered carefully.\n\nPublic agreement can hide private doubts. In a later decision in this chapter, the chair asks volunteers to skip a required equipment check. You will consider how the team can question that instruction before unsafe work begins.',
 [co('Two reasons to follow a group.',['Informational influence: they may know better.', 'Normative influence: I want acceptance.', 'Public agreement can differ from private belief.'],
 'Informational influence means treating other people’s responses as evidence about reality. Sam might think, “Maya agreed, so perhaps she knows we can manage the event.” This is especially relevant when Sam is uncertain.\n\nNormative influence concerns acceptance and the wish to avoid rejection. Sam might think, “I’m worried about the workload, but I don’t want the team to see me as unhelpful.”\n\nBoth influences can operate at once. Sam may doubt their own estimate and fear disappointing the chair.\n\nFor your own group decisions, check the information behind the agreement and whether members can disagree without a social penalty. Those checks help you evaluate the decision without assuming that you know everyone’s private motive.'),
 co('Authority changes the pressure.',['Compliance: responding to a request.','Obedience: responding to an authority’s order.','Make stopping and questioning possible.'],
 'Compliance means responding to a request. If Leo asks Sam to cover a shift and Sam agrees, Sam has complied. Obedience means responding to an authority’s order. Following a chair’s instruction because the chair is in charge illustrates obedience.\n\nA request from an authority can involve both processes. The role may make a demand feel legitimate or encourage someone to think, “The chair is responsible for this decision.” That pressure does not make obedience inevitable. People can resist, and the surrounding conditions matter.\n\nAt the café, an explicit rule allows any volunteer to stop unsafe work. An agreed route for raising the concern gives that volunteer a practical next step after questioning an instruction.')],
 'In Asch’s line-judgment studies, participants compared line lengths after hearing a group give incorrect answers. The studies examined when people would follow a majority despite the visible evidence.\n\nIn Milgram’s studies, an experimenter urged participants to continue a task they believed delivered electric shocks to another person. The studies examined responses to apparent authority in a highly structured setting.\n\nBoth sets of studies raise questions about methods, ethics, and how widely their findings apply. Their results concern particular conditions of influence; they are not universal obedience rates. Some participants resisted, and responses varied with the conditions. Harmful conduct is not inevitable.',
 q('What could reveal concerns before discussion?', ['Have the chair announce the preferred answer.','Collect independent written judgments first.','Ask only experienced members to speak.'],1,['Announcing the chair’s preferred answer first can pull later comments toward it, making independent concerns harder to see.','Independent written judgments let people record a view before seeing which answer has public support.','Experience matters, but other members may have relevant information.']),
 q('The chair requests bypassing a required check. Which process helps?', ['Let the first confident person decide.','Allow anonymous complaints afterward.','Pause and use an agreed safety escalation route.'],2,['Confidence is not evidence of safety.','Later complaints cannot prevent unchecked work now.','An agreed stop-and-escalation route makes questioning possible while the safety decision still matters.']),
 ['Distinguish normative and informational influence.','Why can public agreement overstate private agreement?'],['Normative influence concerns acceptance. Informational influence treats other people’s responses as evidence.','Public agreement can hide private doubt when people copy early answers, suppress concerns, or assume that others have checked.'],
 'For an agreed, low-stakes group choice, have everyone write a view before discussion. Compare the range of initial reasons with what a show of hands might have revealed.',
 [os('12-4-conformity-compliance-and-obedience','Conformity, Compliance, and Obedience'),no('conformity-and-obedience','Conformity and Obedience')])
chapter('The invitation','Attitudes & persuasion','message','#7058A3',
 'Evaluate a message by its evidence, audience needs, and honest description of social norms.',
 'Two invitations. Different reasons to care.',
 'The café needs volunteers. Leo drafts a glossy invitation featuring a local celebrity. Maya’s invitation explains what volunteers can learn, how much time a shift takes, and what support is available.\n\nA hurried passerby might notice the celebrity. A person deciding whether to volunteer regularly needs practical details about the commitment.\n\nAn invitation can attract attention and help someone evaluate a decision. The team needs to consider both jobs, while keeping the invitation honest.',
 [co('Attention and evaluation are different jobs.',['Attitude: an evaluation of something.', 'Elaboration likelihood model: motivation and ability shape careful thinking.', 'Central processing: examine relevant arguments.', 'Peripheral processing: give more weight to cues such as familiarity.'],
 'An attitude is an evaluation: for example, judging volunteering to be worthwhile or burdensome. The elaboration likelihood model describes how motivation and ability affect how carefully someone examines a persuasive message.\n\nCentral processing involves examining relevant arguments. A potential volunteer might compare the shift length, available support, and likely contribution. When someone can and wants to think carefully, argument quality matters more.\n\nPeripheral processing gives more weight to cues, such as a familiar face or an apparently credible speaker. A hurried passerby might feel positive about the café because a favorite local celebrity supports it, without checking the practical details.\n\nThese are differences in how a message is processed, not fixed personality types or two sealed categories. The same person can process different messages more or less carefully.'),
 co('A norm must be true to be useful.',['Descriptive norm: what people do.', 'Injunctive norm: what people approve.', 'A clear request needs an easy next step.'],
 'A descriptive norm concerns what people do. Saying that many residents volunteer communicates a descriptive norm. An injunctive norm concerns approval. Saying that the community values volunteering communicates an injunctive norm.\n\nBoth example claims need evidence. Inventing a norm is misleading. Highlighting how common an unwanted behavior is can sometimes make that behavior seem normal.\n\nWhen you write an invitation, state what is known, explain the contribution needed, and make questions or refusal easy. The reader needs enough information to understand the commitment before choosing.')],
 'Persuasion depends on the audience, topic, source, and outcome being measured. A changed survey answer is not the same outcome as sustained behavior change.\n\nFor the café team, the next task is to evaluate its revised invitation. More clicks show increased attention; they do not establish that potential volunteers understood the commitment or will keep volunteering.\n\nThe team can track comprehension, informed sign-ups, and continued participation. To learn about the volunteers’ experience, ask them whether they understood the effort involved and felt able to decline. Their answers provide information that the organizers’ impressions alone cannot supply.',
 q('A potential volunteer worries about the time commitment. What information would help most?', ['A second celebrity endorsement.','Shift length, support, and an optional trial.','A stronger claim that this is unmissable.'],1,['A celebrity endorsement may attract attention while leaving the volunteer’s time concern unanswered.','Shift length, support, and an optional trial give the volunteer relevant information about the commitment.','Calling the opportunity unmissable adds pressure without answering how much time it requires.']),
 q('Few residents currently volunteer. Which invitation is defensible?', ['“Everyone here already volunteers.”','“Hardly anyone helps—don’t be like them.”','“Try a supported two-hour shift; here is what it involves.”'],2,['Claiming that everyone volunteers invents a descriptive norm that the team cannot substantiate.','Highlighting how few people help can make nonparticipation seem normal. The wording also adds shame.','A supported two-hour trial is a concrete, checkable request. Its effect on informed participation still needs evaluation.']),
 ['What makes central processing more likely?','Distinguish descriptive and injunctive norms.'],['Central processing is more likely when someone is motivated and has the ability, attention, and information to examine the arguments.','Descriptive norms concern what people do. Injunctive norms concern approval.'],
 'Rewrite a low-stakes invitation. State the decision, relevant reason, effort required, and next step. Remove social-proof claims you cannot substantiate.',
 [os('12-3-attitudes-and-persuasion','Attitudes and Persuasion'),no('persuasion-so-easily-fooled','Persuasion: So Easily Fooled')])
chapter('When “we” takes over','Groups & belonging','groups','#A1486D',
 'Identify a plausible group process behind a failure and select a procedure that addresses it.',
 'Two teams stop sharing a table.',
 'Repair volunteers call the welcome desk “the talkers.” The welcome team calls the repair bench “the gatekeepers.” Each team notices its own effort more clearly than the other team’s contribution.\n\nAt a planning meeting, speakers propose increasingly ambitious events. Meanwhile, nobody owns the checklist. Each volunteer assumes someone else is responsible.\n\nThe café now faces several group problems: boundaries between teams, an escalating decision, and unclear task ownership. Each problem may need a different remedy.',
 [co('Identity changes the boundary of “us.”',['Social identity: groups help define who we are.','In-group favoritism: favoring “our” side.','Shared goals can widen the circle.'],
 'Group memberships help people understand who they are and who belongs with them. An in-group is a group someone sees as “us.” Giving repair volunteers first choice of shifts simply because they are “our team” would illustrate in-group favoritism.\n\nFavoritism can produce unequal treatment without explicit hatred of outsiders. At the café, a shared goal—a successful visitor experience—can help the repair and welcome teams see why both contributions matter.\n\nA shared team name alone will not resolve conflicting incentives, unequal workloads, or unequal decision power. If repair volunteers still control the rota, renaming everyone “the café team” leaves that power difference in place. Examine the arrangements that sustain the division.'),
 co('Groups can amplify—or lose—effort.',['Group polarization: discussion can intensify a group’s initial leaning.', 'Social loafing: individual effort can fall in pooled work.', 'Coordination loss: real effort is poorly combined.'],
 'Group polarization means moving toward a more extreme version of a group’s initial leaning. A team initially favoring a small event might finish discussion demanding a week-long festival. Supporting arguments and comparisons with enthusiastic members can contribute. Simply agreeing on the small event would be consensus, not polarization.\n\nSocial loafing means reducing individual effort when working collectively. Someone might check fewer items because their contribution will be hidden in the team’s total.\n\nPoor output can also reflect coordination problems. Two willing volunteers may duplicate one repair while another waits for a missing tool. That wastes genuine effort.\n\nWhen you review an unfinished group task, check effort, tools, sequencing, and roles before choosing a remedy. An unfinished checklist alone does not establish that the volunteers reduced their effort.')],
 'Groupthink describes cases where pressure for agreement interferes with critical discussion. A poor decision alone does not establish that this process occurred.\n\nTo review the café’s planning meeting, look at what happened during the discussion. Which concerns were withheld? Which information was repeated or missed? Were roles or incentives unclear?\n\nThen choose a change that addresses the process you found. Independent estimates can broaden the evidence. Clear roles can improve coordination. Structured disagreement can make concerns easier to express. These changes help the team investigate the decision without pretending to know every member’s private thoughts.',
 q('The shared checklist is unfinished. What is a useful first change?', ['Agree on owners, deadlines, and needed support.','Tell everyone their commitment is disappointing.','Copy more people onto every task.'],0,['Agreed owners, deadlines, and support make responsibility clearer and help people coordinate the work.','Telling everyone to be more committed still leaves the team unsure who owns each checklist task.','Copying more people onto a task can leave each volunteer assuming another recipient will handle it.']),
 q('An event budget keeps escalating without checking demand. What helps?', ['Ask members to show stronger enthusiasm.','Gather independent estimates and contrary evidence.','Open with the chair’s preferred budget.'],1,['Rewarding public enthusiasm can encourage visible commitment without improving the demand estimate.','Independent estimates and contrary evidence give the group information beyond its existing enthusiasm.','The chair’s opening budget can become an anchor: a starting number that pulls later estimates toward it, even when demand has not been checked.']),
 ['How does polarization differ from consensus?','How can a willing group still perform poorly?'],['Consensus is agreement. Polarization is movement toward a more extreme position from an initial leaning.','A willing group can still lose output through poor sequencing, duplicated work, missing tools, or unclear roles.'],
 'Choose a shared project you know well. Map each task, its owner, what must happen before it can start, and when progress will be checked. Identify one plausible obstacle and a check that could distinguish it from another explanation before proposing a remedy.',
 [os('12-4-conformity-compliance-and-obedience','Conformity, Compliance, and Obedience'),os('12-5-prejudice-and-discrimination','Prejudice and Discrimination')])
chapter('The invisible doorway','Prejudice & discrimination','door','#216B88',
 'Distinguish stereotypes, prejudice, and discrimination and locate a procedural barrier to access.',
 '“Anyone can join.” The form says otherwise.',
 'The café says everyone is welcome. Yet recruitment runs through existing friends, meetings happen during working hours, and the application assumes that people own specialist tools.\n\nA newcomer is treated as inexperienced before anyone checks the applicant’s skills. The barrier may involve a judgment about the person, a recruitment rule, or both.\n\nTo understand unequal access, examine beliefs, attitudes, individual decisions, and procedures. Good intentions alone do not tell us how the process works.',
 [co('Belief, feeling, and action are distinct.',['Stereotype: a generalized group belief.','Prejudice: a group attitude, often involving dislike.','Discrimination: unequal treatment based on group membership.'],
 'A stereotype is a generalized belief about a group: for example, assuming that every newcomer lacks repair skills. Prejudice is an attitude toward a group, often involving dislike: resenting newcomers before meeting them. Discrimination is unequal treatment based on group membership: rejecting an applicant because they are a newcomer, without checking relevant skills.\n\nThese examples concern beliefs, attitudes, and actions respectively. The three can overlap, but evidence about one does not establish everything about the others.\n\nTo evaluate the recruitment process, compare the actual decisions and requirements applied to applicants. Good intentions alone do not establish that the process is fair.\n\nAssessing an organizer’s beliefs is a different question. A decision is evidence to examine, but one action does not reveal the organizer’s entire belief system.'),
 co('Change the encounter and the structure.',['Intergroup contact: interaction between members of different groups.', 'Support cooperation, shared goals, equal status, and backing from organizers.', 'Procedural change: remove an unnecessary entry requirement.', 'Evaluate whether people can actually participate.'],
 'Intergroup contact means interaction between members of different groups. It can reduce prejudice on average, though bringing people together does not guarantee improvement. Cooperation, common goals, equal status within the encounter, and institutional support are important conditions to consider.\n\nFor example, experienced volunteers and newcomers could work together on a repair, share decisions, and receive support from the coordinator. If newcomers only watch while established members control the tools, being in the same room offers less equal participation.\n\nContact also leaves some barriers untouched. A friendly social event does not change a rule requiring applicants to own expensive tools. Removing that requirement, where unnecessary, is a procedural change. Evaluate whether people can actually enter and participate.')],
 'An explicit attitude is an evaluation someone reports directly. An implicit association is a link between ideas that may be measured indirectly, such as through the speed of responses in a sorting task. Discriminatory behavior concerns how people are actually treated.\n\nWhen interpreting a participant’s implicit-association score, remember that it is not a complete diagnosis of that person’s character or a forecast of every action.\n\nWhen evaluating a recruitment change, inspect access and participation: who can apply, progress, and remain involved? Include follow-up and the experiences of applicants. An organizer saying “I am fair” cannot establish that those outcomes are fair.\n\nInterpret small samples cautiously and protect privacy. Investigating unequal access does not justify collecting unnecessary personal information.',
 q('How should the team investigate unequal access?', ['Ask whether organizers consider themselves fair.','Trace who can enter each stage and where people drop out.','Add a fairness slogan to the poster.'],1,['Organizers’ beliefs about their own fairness do not establish who can use the recruitment process.','Tracing each stage can reveal access barriers that intentions and slogans miss.','The slogan does not change the entry requirements.']),
 q('Friend referrals become an open skills-based application. How can improvement be assessed?', ['Check whether organizers feel more inclusive.','Compare the friendliness of form wording.','Track applications, progression, and participation using relevant criteria.'],2,['Organizers feeling more inclusive is a reaction to the change. It does not establish that access improved.','Wording can help, but outcomes still matter.','Applications, progression, and participation can show where barriers remain. Interpret those outcomes with context and privacy safeguards.']),
 ['Give an example of discrimination without an openly hostile statement.','Why might a contact event leave unequal access unchanged?'],['An irrelevant requirement or restricted network can exclude a group without an openly hostile statement.','Contact can leave unequal access unchanged when decision power, incentives, status differences, or procedures remain the same.'],
 'Review a process you are authorized to examine. Identify an unnecessary barrier, propose a change, and select a fair outcome measure without collecting unnecessary personal data.',
 [os('12-5-prejudice-and-discrimination','Prejudice and Discrimination'),no('prejudice-discrimination-and-stereotyping','Prejudice, Discrimination, and Stereotyping')])
chapter('A place beside someone','Attraction & relationships','relationship','#7058A3',
 'Explain how contact opportunities and reciprocal responsiveness can support closeness without guaranteeing it.',
 'Friendship begins with the quiet shift.',
 'Sam and Leo take the quiet Thursday session. At first, they discuss only spare parts. Over several weeks, they discover a shared interest in old radios.\n\nLeo mentions a difficult week. Sam listens before offering a solution, and Leo feels heard.\n\nTheir shared shift created opportunities to meet. Their common interest supported conversation. Sam’s responsive listening supported closeness. None of those conditions gave either person a right to the other’s friendship.',
 [co('Opportunity shapes who becomes familiar.',['Proximity creates opportunities to meet.', 'Mere exposure effect: familiarity can increase liking in some conditions.', 'Similarity can make interaction easier.'],
 'Daily settings influence whom we have opportunities to meet. Sharing the Thursday shift makes Sam and Leo more familiar to each other. Their interest in old radios gives them something to discuss.\n\nThe mere exposure effect refers to cases where repeated encounters increase liking. A face that initially feels unfamiliar may become more comfortable to see. Repetition can also intensify annoyance or threat, so more contact is not automatically better.\n\nSimilar interests and values can make connection easier. Contact, familiarity, and similarity do not guarantee attraction, compatibility, or a healthy relationship. Sam and Leo’s shared shift creates opportunities for friendship; it does not create an obligation to become friends. Someone’s expressed boundary still matters.'),
 co('Closeness is a reciprocal process.',['Disclosure: sharing something personal.', 'Responsiveness: helping someone feel understood.', 'Reciprocity: mutual consideration over time.', 'Attachment labels are not diagnoses; willingness and boundaries still matter.'],
 'Disclosure means sharing something personal. Leo mentioning a difficult week is a disclosure. Responsiveness means responding in a way that helps someone feel understood and cared for. Sam could listen, check what Leo means, and ask whether advice would help.\n\nDisclosure can support intimacy when it receives an understanding response. Reciprocity means that consideration can flow both ways over time; it does not require matching secrets on demand or treating favors as debts. Trust, willingness, fairness, and commitment also matter.\n\nPeople differ in how secure or anxious they feel about closeness. Attachment labels describe patterns studied in relationships; they are not diagnoses.\n\nWhen Sam declines an invitation, Leo needs to respond to Sam’s stated preference. One refusal does not establish Sam’s attachment pattern or justify ignoring that preference.')],
 'Much relationship research examines associations or asks people to report their own experiences. Self-reports provide information about those experiences, but people may remember events differently or answer in ways they prefer others to see. Samples may also represent only particular cultures or relationship forms.\n\nSuppose similar partners report greater satisfaction. That association does not, by itself, establish that making two people more similar would improve their relationship. Other factors might contribute to both similarity and satisfaction.\n\nTo apply that research to Sam and Leo, first ask about their actual experiences and preferences. Their conversations may reveal relevant details that a group average cannot settle. A research pattern cannot decide what either person should want.',
 q('A newcomer hopes to make friends. What is a respectful starting point?', ['Offer a shared activity and notice reciprocal interest.','Share increasingly private details until closeness develops.','Keep arranging contact after invitations are declined.'],0,['Offering a shared activity creates an opportunity to connect. Notice whether the other person also chooses to participate and shows interest.','Disclosure without mutual readiness can feel intrusive.','A refusal is information about a boundary, not a barrier to overcome.']),
 q('Sam declines an invitation and needs time alone. What should Leo do?', ['Diagnose an avoidant attachment style.','Accept the boundary and leave future contact optional.','Increase contact to use the familiarity effect.'],1,['One declined invitation does not establish an attachment pattern.','Accepting the invitation refusal respects Sam’s stated preference without inventing a diagnosis.','The familiarity effect is not a reason to ignore Sam’s expressed boundary.']),
 ['How can proximity affect friendship?','Why is disclosure alone insufficient?'],['Proximity changes opportunities to interact and discover shared interests.','Disclosure alone is insufficient because willingness, responsiveness, trust, context, and mutual boundaries also matter.'],
 'During a willing everyday conversation, ask an open question. Summarize what you heard and invite the person to correct your summary before offering advice. Afterwards, reflect on your own responses and the feedback the person actually gave.',
 [os('12-7-prosocial-behavior','Prosocial Behavior'),no('attraction-and-beauty','Attraction and Beauty')])
chapter('Who will do something?','Aggression, helping & cooperation','help','#A1486D',
 'Identify barriers to helping and design a specific, safe response with clear responsibility.',
 'A crash. A full room. A moment of hesitation.',
 'A shelf falls during a busy café session, and a visitor needs help. People hesitate while checking everyone else’s reaction.\n\nMaya names the problem. Maya asks Leo to contact the appropriate emergency service and clears space for a trained helper.\n\nLater, two volunteers argue about blame. The team needs to review both the immediate response and the conditions supporting cooperation. Good intentions need a clear, safe path to action.',
 [co('Helping has several decision points.',['Bystander effect: others’ presence can reduce an individual’s chance of helping.', 'Diffusion of responsibility: “Someone else will act.”', 'Pluralistic ignorance: “Everyone looks calm, so perhaps no help is needed.”', 'Help depends on context, skills, safety, and support.'],
 'The bystander effect describes conditions where the presence of others reduces an individual’s likelihood of helping. Diffusion of responsibility is one possible process. A concerned volunteer might think, “There are plenty of people here; someone else will act,” and feel less personally responsible.\n\nPluralistic ignorance concerns interpreting other people’s reactions. Leo might read Sam’s outward calm as reassurance that no help is needed, even while Sam is privately uncertain. In that case, Leo has mistaken Sam’s appearance for Sam’s private assessment.\n\nDiffusion concerns who should act; pluralistic ignorance concerns how the need for action is interpreted. Both could contribute to hesitation after the shelf falls. In other emergencies, people provide essential support and increase the chance that someone helps. The effect depends on the situation.'),
 co('Make constructive action easier.',['Aggression: behavior intended to harm.','De-escalation: lower threat and preserve safety.','Cooperation: align goals, roles, and feedback.'],
 'Aggression is behavior intended to harm. Anger is a feeling; aggression involves an intention to harm through behavior. Someone can feel angry about the fallen shelf and still respond constructively.\n\nFrustration, perceived threat, situational pressure, and learned expectations about how conflicts are handled can contribute to aggression. Those influences do not make harm inevitable or remove responsibility.\n\nDe-escalation means reducing tension and the risk of harm. At the café, space to calm down, clear conduct rules, and a trained person to contact may help. Evaluate these arrangements rather than treating them as guarantees.\n\nCooperation also needs clear contributions, a way to report problems, and a fair division of benefits and burdens. Naming who checks a replacement shelf turns a general wish to cooperate into a specific responsibility.')],
 'Laboratory studies may measure aggression through controlled tasks, such as choosing an unpleasant noise for another participant. Such measures are imperfect indicators of serious violence outside the laboratory.\n\nBystander findings also vary with danger, relationships, and available support. “Crowds don’t help” hides those differences and cannot explain every incident.\n\nFor a real emergency, follow local guidance and your training. A social psychology explanation does not replace first-aid instruction. Evaluate practical changes by whether appropriate, safe help becomes more available. A team saying it cares does not establish that its response plan works.',
 q('Why could concerned bystanders hesitate?', ['Concern disappears when groups become large.','People may be uncertain and assume someone else will act.','Hesitation proves nobody cares.'],1,['Group size does not determine concern on its own.','People may be unsure whether help is needed, or think someone else is responsible for acting.','A person may care and still hesitate because they do not know what happened, lack the skills to help, or fear making things worse.']),
 q('Which preparation makes future help more likely?', ['Tell everyone to be more caring.','Assume the most senior person handles all incidents.','Agree on response roles, training, and a call-for-help route.'],2,['Asking everyone to care more leaves the required action and responsibility unclear.','The most senior person may be absent or lack the relevant training. Assuming that person will act leaves a gap in the plan.','Roles, training, and a call-for-help route address ability and responsibility as well as good intentions.']),
 ['Distinguish diffusion of responsibility from pluralistic ignorance.','Imagine you are reviewing the café’s response to the fallen shelf. Choose two ideas from earlier chapters and explain how each would improve the review.'],['Diffusion of responsibility concerns who should act. Pluralistic ignorance concerns how people interpret others’ reactions.','For an incident review, separate observations from blame, compare possible causes, invite independent accounts, or assign clear roles. These draw on chapters one, two, five, and seven.'],
 'Design a helping plan for a low-risk shared task: signal, named role, safe action, and feedback. Explain how context, evidence, influence, and group structure shape your plan.',
 [os('12-6-aggression','Aggression'),os('12-7-prosocial-behavior','Prosocial Behavior'),no('helping-and-prosocial-behavior','Helping and Prosocial Behavior')])

STAGES=['Story','Your decision','First lens','Second lens','Evidence check','New situation','Recall','Field practice']
for i,c in enumerate(C):
 c['number']=i+1
 def s(kind,title,points,text,**kw):return dict(kind=kind,title=title,points=points,text=text,**kw)
 c['slides']=[s('story',c['hook'],['An original story at the Common Ground repair café.'],c['story']),s('choice','What would you do?',[], 'Before the explanation, make your own choice.\n\n'+c['quizzes'][0]['prompt']+'\n\nConsider the consequences of each option.',quiz=c['quizzes'][0])]
 c['slides'] += [s('concept',x['title'],x['points'],x['text']) for x in c['concepts']]
 c['slides'] += [s('evidence','What can we really conclude?',['What the research supports','Where the claim needs restraint'],c['evidence']),s('transfer','Try the idea somewhere new.',[], 'Apply the idea to a new decision.\n\n'+c['quizzes'][1]['prompt']+'\n\nChoose an action and explain why it addresses the problem.',quiz=c['quizzes'][1]),s('recall','Close your notes. Explain it.',c['recall'],'Answer both prompts from memory.\n\nFirst. '+c['recall'][0]+'\n\nSecond. '+c['recall'][1]+'\n\nAnswer each prompt first. Then give a brief example of your own that illustrates one of your answers. Try both prompts before revealing the suggested answers.',answers=c['answers']),s('practice','Carry one idea into your week.',[c['field']], 'Here is your field practice.\n\n'+c['field']+'\n\nReturn tomorrow and in a week. On each visit, answer the recall questions without notes and use a different example.')]
 for j,x in enumerate(c['slides']):x.update(id=f'c{i+1:02d}s{j+1:02d}',stage=STAGES[j])
# Keep existing slide identities and links when adding a new learning step.
c=C[0]
c['slides'][4]['title']='One observer, two explanations.'
c['slides'].insert(5,dict(id='c01s05b',kind='concept',stage='Check an explanation',title='Test the explanation.',
 points=['Describe: Maya arrived late; “doesn’t care” is a proposed cause.',
         'Compare: look across shifts, settings, and exceptions.',
         'Check: ask Maya and test plausible alternatives.',
         'Revise: change your confidence when the evidence changes.'],
 text='Return to Maya’s late arrival at the café. Leo has proposed that she did not care about the project. How could he check that explanation?\n\nBegin with what was observed: Maya arrived after the start. Leo’s claim that she did not care adds a cause. Ask Maya about the delay before deciding which explanation fits.\n\nCompare repeated occasions and settings. Is Maya late across different shifts, or only when a particular obstacle is present? A personal tendency becomes more plausible when the behavior recurs across varied circumstances. One episode is a weak basis for that conclusion.\n\nCheck alternatives. A broken lift or helping a neighbor could explain this delay. Identify what evidence would strengthen or weaken each account, then revise your confidence.\n\nIf you are reviewing someone else’s reasoning, ask what that person observed and considered. Disagreement alone does not establish bias. When you form your own interpretation, use these same steps to test it.'))
